 ## Background
-Simulation-based volume data generation has roots in computational fluid dynamics (CFD) and scientific visualization. Early methods focused on grid-based Eulerian approaches for smoke and fire, such as the MacCormack advection scheme described in GPU Gems [S2, unofficial]. Lagrangian methods like Position-Based Fluids emerged to address stability issues in particle-based simulations [S2, unofficial]. In scientific visualization, tools like Smokeview used massless particles and volumetric rendering to visualize Fire Dynamics Simulation (FDS) data [S3, unofficial]. More recently, kinematic simulations have been used in astrophysics to visualize galaxy evolution within cosmological contexts [S4]. The integration of these simulations with real-time rendering pipelines, such as GPU-based voxel rendering [S1, unofficial] and Gaussian Splatting [S3, unofficial], represents a shift towards interactive, physically-based visual effects.
+Simulation-based volume data generation has roots in computational fluid dynamics (CFD) and scientific visualization [G]. Early methods focused on grid-based Eulerian approaches for smoke and fire, such as the MacCormack advection scheme and reaction coordinate tracking described in GPU Gems [S2, unofficial]. Lagrangian methods like Position-Based Fluids emerged to address stability issues in particle-based simulations [S2, unofficial]. In scientific visualization, tools like Smokeview used massless particles and volumetric rendering to visualize Fire Dynamics Simulation (FDS) data [S3, unofficial]. Kinematic simulations have been applied in astrophysics to visualize galaxy evolution within cosmological contexts using double-precision arithmetic [S4]. Procedural fire systems also integrate particle simulations for macro-level effects like wind and thermal buoyancy, combined with pixel-shader noise for micro-level flicker [S5].
 
